<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Jean-Jacques Acaau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1846">1846</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Jean-Jacques Acaau (died 1846) was a Haitian peasant leader and former army officer who led the piquets, an uprising of black smallholders in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, in 1844. Demanding land, cheaper imported goods and justice for the poor against the mixed-race elite, the revolt helped bring down President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-riviere-herard">Charles Rivière-Hérard</a>. Acaau later rebelled again and took his own life in 1846.</p></div>
